<!-- COMMON MISTAKES -->
<section class="section" aria-labelledby="mistakes-heading">
  <div class="container">
    <div class="split-grid">
      <div>
        <h2 style="margin-bottom:1rem;">Mistakes We See When Businesses Try This Themselves</h2>
        <p style="color:#42474e; font-size:1.0625rem; line-height:1.85; margin-bottom:1.25rem;">Plenty of teams start automating on their own with a tool like Zapier or Make, and a lot of that early work is worth keeping. The problems tend to show up in the same few places.</p>
        <p style="color:#42474e; font-size:1.0625rem; line-height:1.85; margin-bottom:1.25rem;"><strong>Automating the wrong step first.</strong> It's tempting to automate whatever is easiest to connect, not whatever costs the most time. That usually means the first automation someone builds saves ten minutes a week instead of ten hours.</p>
        <p style="color:#42474e; font-size:1.0625rem; line-height:1.85; margin-bottom:1.25rem;"><strong>No plan for exceptions.</strong> A workflow built for the clean, typical case breaks the first time a customer submits a form with a missing field or a supplier sends an invoice in a new layout. Without a way to catch and handle exceptions, someone ends up manually fixing broken automations, which defeats the point of building one.</p>
        <p style="color:#42474e; font-size:1.0625rem; line-height:1.85;"><strong>Nobody owns it after launch.</strong> An automation built by whoever had spare time that month tends to quietly break when a connected app changes its interface or a field gets renamed, and nobody notices until something downstream goes wrong.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="split-media">
        <img src="https://images.unsplash.com/photo-1519389950473-47ba0277781c?w=800&q=80&auto=format&fit=crop" alt="Team reviewing an automation workflow together" loading="lazy" width="800" height="600" />
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

